---
import Content from '../layouts/Content.astro';
import { company, site, power, solar } from '../data/site';

/* Derived from the record rather than retyped, so the arithmetic can never
   drift from it. Apparent power only: sqrt(3) x 208 V x 3,000 A. */
const serviceKva = Math.round(Math.sqrt(3) * 208 * 3000 / 1000);
---

<Content
  title={`Power — the service that is standing, and what it means | ${company.full}`}
  description={`Buildings A, B and C at ${site.designation} share an owner-reported ${power.service} distribution originating at Building C. Usable IT capacity follows electrical, cooling and commissioning review. Tract 3 holds ${solar.tractAcres} acres for planned solar and storage.`}
  kicker="The power story"
  heading="Start with the power."
  lede={`Buildings A, B and C share an owner-reported ${power.service} distribution originating at Building C. That is a real head start, and it is not the same thing as available capacity — this page keeps the two apart, because most of the industry's power claims do not.`}
  meta={[
    { label: 'Shared service', value: power.service, note: power.serviceScope, tone: 'gold' },
    { label: 'Apparent rating', value: power.serviceApparent, note: 'Arithmetic, not a nameplate' },
    { label: 'Tenant allocation', value: 'On review', note: power.allocation, tone: 'signal' },
    { label: 'Planned energy', value: `${solar.tractAcres} acres`, note: 'Tract 3, solar and storage' },
  ]}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="queue" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">01</span><span class="eyebrow__t">The problem</span></p>
    <h2 id="queue">Four things that get called "power"</h2>

    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>
          <strong>Existing distribution</strong> is what is physically on the parcel:
          {' '}{power.service}, one shared rating originating at Building C and serving
          Buildings A, B and C. <strong>Planned generation</strong> is Tract 3, where
          {' '}{solar.tractAcres} acres are set aside for solar and storage that does not
          exist yet.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          <strong>Storage design</strong> is a separate exercise again — a power rating is
          not usable energy. And <strong>approved tenant allocation</strong> is the only
          one of the four you can actually buy, and the only one this site will not put a
          number on until an engineer has. Conflating them is how a service rating becomes
          a capacity claim.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="versus" data-reveal>
      <div class="versus__side">
        <p class="versus__label">Apparent service rating</p>
        <p class="versus__value">{serviceKva.toLocaleString()} kVA</p>
        <p class="versus__note">
          √3 × 208 V × 3,000 A. Arithmetic from the stated service current — not a
          transformer nameplate, not real power, and not something to multiply by a
          convenient factor and call engineered capacity.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="versus__side is-answer">
        <p class="versus__label">Usable IT capacity</p>
        <p class="versus__value">On review</p>
        <p class="versus__note">
          {power.allocation}. Continuous-load limits, utility allocation, feeder ratings,
          cooling, existing loads and redundancy all sit between the figure on the left and
          a number you could build against.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="btm" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">02</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Behind the meter</span></p>
    <h2 id="btm">What is actually on the parcel</h2>

    <p>
      Three things are established, and each one has a boundary worth stating.
    </p>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Shared distribution</h3>
        <p>
          {power.service}, {power.serviceOrigin.toLowerCase()}. {power.serviceScope}. The
          house and office run on a separate {power.houseOfficeService} supply and are
          excluded from data-center floor area and capacity.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Existing cooling</h3>
        <p>
          {power.hvac} Nominal tonnage is not IT kilowatts, cooling in one building is not
          automatically available in another, and two units are not automatically N+1.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Planned energy</h3>
        <p>
          Tract 3 holds {solar.tractAcres} acres for planned solar and storage.
          {' '}{power.storage} are reported at {power.storageRating}; that is a power
          rating, and the usable energy behind it is not established.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" style="margin-top: var(--s-6);">
      <p>
        <strong>The one sentence that matters.</strong> A service standing on owned land
        is a genuine head start on a greenfield build — and it is a head start on a
        fit-out, not a finished one. {site.designation} is offering a
        {' '}{site.powerOn} for first compute and an engineering review to establish what
        the buildings can take, rather than a capacity number derived from a service
        rating.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="service" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">03</span><span class="eyebrow__t">The service</span></p>
    <h2 id="service">What is standing on the parcel</h2>

    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>Shared service</dt><dd>{power.service}<span class="note">{power.serviceScope}. {power.serviceOrigin}.</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Apparent rating</dt><dd>{power.serviceApparent}<span class="note">{power.serviceApparentNote}</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Transformer nameplate</dt><dd><span class="todo">{power.transformerNameplate}</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>House / office</dt><dd>{power.houseOfficeService}<span class="note">{power.houseOfficeNote}</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Cooling</dt><dd>{power.hvac}<span class="note">{power.hvacUnresolved}</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tenant allocation</dt><dd>{power.allocation}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Energy rate</dt><dd><span class="todo">{power.rate}</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Interconnection</dt><dd>{power.interconnection}<span class="note">{power.interconnectionNote}</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Utility of record</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: serving utility / cooperative name and account status]</span></dd></div>
    </dl>

    <blockquote class="pull">
      <p>A service rating is not an allocation.</p>
      <p>
        The arithmetic above converts a stated service current into apparent power, and
        that is all it does. Turning it into a kilowatt figure a tenant could build
        against needs a single-line diagram, a transformer nameplate, feeder ratings, the
        serving utility's allocation, the existing loads and a cooling review — none of
        which this site has published, because it does not yet have them.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <figure class="photo" style="margin-top: var(--s-6);">
      <div class="photo__frame">
        <svg class="photo__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="2.5" y="6" width="19" height="14" rx="2" />
          <circle cx="12" cy="13" r="3.5" />
          <path d="M8.5 6l1.4-2.2h4.2L15.5 6" />
          <path d="M3 3l18 18" />
        </svg>
        <p class="photo__state">Photograph not taken yet</p>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        <b>[PHOTO NEEDED]</b> The transformer on its pad, and the
        service entrance into Building 1. For a buyer evaluating this page, this is the
        single most load-bearing photograph on the whole site — it is the proof that the
        power claim is not a plan.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="solar" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">04</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Generation and storage</span></p>
    <h2 id="solar">Planned solar and storage</h2>

    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>
          {solar.publicCopy}
        </p>
        <p>
          The range below is a screening extrapolation and nothing more. It applies
          published array-area benchmarks — from a study whose sample is larger
          utility-scale plants — to an assumed buildable fraction of an unsurveyed
          parcel. These are alternative layouts, not capacities that add together, and
          the illustrative AC outputs exceed the existing service rating, which is
          precisely why a campus electrical and interconnection design has to come first.
        </p>
        <p>
          {company.parent} builds LFP cabinets and {company.name} is the data center arm of
          the same company. What that is worth in installed cost is a real question and an
          undocumented one:
          {' '}<span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: documented related-party equipment cost, warranty and service obligations]</span>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="figures">
        <div>
          <dt>Tract 3</dt>
          <dd class="is-gold">{solar.tractAcres} acres<span class="sub">Set aside for planned solar and storage</span></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Screening range</dt>
          <dd>{solar.range}<span class="sub">{solar.status}. Not surveyed capacity and not utility-approved generation.</span></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Storage</dt>
          <dd>{power.storageRating}<span class="sub">{power.storageNote}</span></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Interconnection</dt>
          <dd>Unresolved<span class="sub">{power.interconnectionNote}</span></dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top: var(--s-6);">
      Sizing detail a buyer will want before modelling anything —
      {' '}<span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: battery cabinet count, usable kWh, C-rate and expected daily cycles]</span>,
      {' '}<span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: solar array configuration, tilt, expected annual yield in MWh]</span>,
      and <span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: construction and commissioning dates for the solar array and the battery plant]</span>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="economics" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">05</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Economics</span></p>
    <h2 id="economics">What the power costs</h2>

    <p>
      There is no published rate here yet, so there is no worked example either. The
      previous version of this page multiplied an estimated load by a rate and an
      assumed 100% utilisation and presented the product as an annual energy cost. Every
      input to that was an estimate, and the output read like a quote.
    </p>

    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>Energy rate</dt><dd><span class="todo">{power.rate}</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Demand charges</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: demand charge structure from the serving utility]</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Facility overhead</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: measured PUE — a design figure is not a measured one, and there is no operating history to measure yet]</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tenant billing</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: whether energy is passed through at cost, blended into a per-kW rate, or metered]</span></dd></div>
    </dl>

    <p>
      When those four exist, the arithmetic goes here in full so you can check it rather
      than take a "competitive rates" claim on trust. Until then, an estimate dressed as a
      figure would be worse than the gap.
    </p>


    <p style="margin-top: var(--s-5);">
      This is arithmetic on two published numbers at full utilisation, for IT load only.
      It is not a quote, it is not a tenant price, and it does not include cooling
      overhead, network, or space. Tenant commercial terms are on the
      {' '}<a href="/colocation">colocation page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="is-end" aria-labelledby="unclaimed" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">06</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Not yet claimed</span></p>
    <h2 id="unclaimed">What we are not claiming about resilience</h2>

    <p>
      A behind-the-meter position solves schedule. It does not by itself make a claim
      about uptime, and we are not going to imply one. Every item below is a question a
      serious tenant asks in the first meeting, and every one of them is open.
    </p>

    <ul class="bullets">
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: UPS topology and ride-through duration for the IT load]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: standby generation — whether any is installed, fuel type, run time]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: electrical redundancy — N, N+1, or 2N, at the distribution and UPS levels]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: whether the site targets a formal resilience classification, and which]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: switchgear arrangement and single points of failure between the transformer and the racks]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: uptime SLA offered to tenants, and the service credit schedule behind it]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: maintenance windows and how they are notified]</span></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="/colocation">Colocation terms</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/site">See the site</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Content>
